{"kind":"request","text":"When the adversarial spike fails in CI, the log says \"fail\" and nothing else, and the evidence that would explain it is deleted with the runner.\n\nMeasured today from two real failures. Runs 31382170054 and 31382676880 both failed at `make spike`. The entire diagnostic output is one line: `fail: wrote .../evidence.json, .../SPIKE-RESULTS.md, and .../SPIKE-RESULTS.md`. spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go:154 prints the status word and three paths and never names which case failed. The workflow runs `make spike` with no artifact upload, so those three files exist on the runner for the remainder of the job and are then thrown away.\n\nThe consequence is that a spike failure cannot be diagnosed after the fact by anyone, including the person who caused it. I tried: the six cases are Concurrent retry and failover, Rebinding attacks, Nexus crash with live ephemera, Unauthorized fetch across a domain, Snapshot/watch race, and Conflicting multi-log custody transition, and I cannot tell you which of them went red on those runs. The information existed for about a second.\n\nBoth failures were also provably not caused by their commits: 3c6d676c changed four lines of README.md and c6128ff1 changed sixteen, and the next README-only commit passed. So the spike is intermittently failing on unrelated pushes, which is exactly the situation where you most need to know which property broke — an intermittent failure in an adversarial security case is either a flaky harness or a real race, and those are not the same thing at all.\n\nThis is worth more than the ordinary flaky-test complaint. These six cases exist to establish specific security properties the repository claims. A gate that says only \"one of your six security properties failed, and I have discarded the evidence\" is close to no gate at all: the honest reading of a red spike run today is \"something may be broken and we cannot find out\", and the tempting reading is \"spike is flaky again\".\n\nFiling rather than implementing, deliberately. Hugh asked me to check CI failures and I have; changing the workflow is his call to make and I would rather it be requested than done. The two halves are separable and the second is the one that matters.","body":{"conditions":"A failing `make spike` run names the case or cases that failed, on stderr, so the CI log alone identifies which security property broke; the generated evidence.json and SPIKE-RESULTS.md are retained from a CI run that fails, by workflow artifact upload or another durable route, so a failure can be diagnosed after the runner is gone; a passing run is not made noisier by either change; the retention decision names what is kept and for how long, since these files describe adversarial cases; the change is demonstrated by forcing one case to fail and reading the resulting CI log and artifact rather than by reasoning about it; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}